.TH ODYSIA 1 "July 25, 2026" "Odysia 0.5.0-beta5" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
odysia \- explore Linux kernel source code and documentation
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B odysia
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Odysia
is a GTK3 desktop source explorer for the Linux kernel and other supported
source trees.  It builds a local, navigable index of declarations,
relationships, source locations, source snippets, and documentation.
.PP
The application parses source files concurrently, keeps the interface
responsive during indexing, and can save or restore a complete index in an
SQLite database.  Source analysis is local and does not upload files or
require an AI service.
.SH WORKFLOW
.TP
.B 1.
Choose
.B File > Select Source Tree
and select a Linux kernel or supported source directory.
.TP
.B 2.
Optionally choose
.B Actions > Settings
to change the source parser thread count.
.TP
.B 3.
Choose
.B Actions > Index
to discover source and documentation files and build the symbol tree.
.TP
.B 4.
Filter or sort the tree, select an entry, inspect its details and source, and
follow clickable symbol relationships.
.TP
.B 5.
Choose
.B File > Save SQLite Index
to preserve the index for a later session.
.SH INTERFACE
.SS File menu
.TP
.B Select Source Tree
Select the root directory to index.  Pressing Enter in the source-tree entry
also starts indexing.
.TP
.B Open SQLite Index
Load a previously saved Odysia index, including symbols, child relationships,
relations, documentation, source metadata, and the original root path.
.TP
.B Save SQLite Index
Write the complete loaded index to an SQLite database.  Saving to an existing
file replaces its Odysia tables transactionally.
.TP
.B Clear Data
Cancel active work and remove the loaded index, filter, tree rows, details,
and progress state from the application.
.TP
.B Quit
Close the application and cancel active indexing or build monitoring.
.SS Actions menu
.TP
.B Index
Build an index of the selected source tree.  Discovery, source parsing,
documentation parsing, and tree construction are shown as separate progress
stages.
.TP
.B Build Kernel
Run
.B make \-j1 V=1
in the selected source tree.  The build dialog merges standard error with
standard output, streams the log, and displays the current compilation unit.
The source tree must already be configured for the intended native or cross
build.
.TP
.B Stop
Cancel active source indexing or stop an active monitored kernel build.  During
indexing this is the only available application action; other controls remain
disabled until cancellation completes.  It remains disabled during SQLite
loading.
.TP
.B Settings
Set the bounded GLib source parser worker count.  The default is one worker per
detected logical CPU.  The CPU, 2x, and 3x presets select one, two, or three
times that count.  Separate font selectors control the detail and source panes.
Apply saves all three settings across launches.  Reset Defaults selects one
worker per detected logical CPU,
.B Sans 11
for details, and
.B Monospace 11
for source; select Apply to save the reset values.  Larger worker counts are not
necessarily faster.
.SS Help menu
.TP
.B About
Display the program name and package version.
.SH SYMBOL TREE
The tree hierarchy is programming language, construct kind, initial letter,
and symbol.  Nonalphabetic names are placed under
.BR # .
Owned fields, parameters, local variables, and enumerators remain beneath
their parent declaration.
.PP
The tree contains columns for the symbol-kind icon,
.BR Docs ,
.BR Symbol ,
.BR Type ,
and
.BR "Directory / Source" .
A warning-style exclamation icon in the
.B Docs
column means the entry has inline documentation or is mentioned in an indexed
external documentation file.
.PP
Functions are displayed with parentheses, for example
.BR schedule() .
The filter searches names, source paths, signatures, types, documentation, and
symbol kinds.  Results can be sorted by name, source line, or symbol kind.
.PP
During source indexing, SQLite loading, and symbol-tree construction, inputs,
tree rows, text panes, and unrelated menu actions are disabled.  Stop remains
available during source indexing and its tree construction.  Progress and
status remain visible, and controls are restored only when the operation
finishes, is cancelled, or fails.
.SH SUPPORTED INPUTS
.SS C and headers
Files ending in
.B .c
or
.B .h
receive the deepest analysis.  Odysia extracts functions, structs, unions,
enums, enumerators, typedefs, globals, fields, parameters, local variables,
function pointers, return types, calls, and common C99, GNU, and Linux kernel
declaration patterns.
.SS Other kernel source formats
Declaration-oriented scanners index:
.TP
.B Rust (.rs)
Functions, structs, enums, unions, traits, modules, aliases, constants,
statics, and macro rules.
.TP
.B Assembly (.S, .s, .asm)
Kernel function-entry macros, assembly macros, and labels.
.TP
.B Python (.py), shell (.sh), Perl (.pl, .pm), and AWK (.awk)
Functions, classes, packages, and other principal declarations.  Extensionless
scripts using recognized shebang lines are also discovered.
.TP
.B Device tree (.dts, .dtsi)
Labeled and unlabeled device nodes.
.TP
.B Coccinelle (.cocci)
Named semantic-patch rules.
.TP
.B Lex and Yacc (.l, .y)
Named grammar rules.
.TP
.B Linker scripts (.lds, .lds.S)
Named labels and sections.
.TP
.B Make and Kbuild
Targets and variables in
.BR Makefile* ,
.BR Kbuild* ,
and
.B .mk
files.
.TP
.B Kconfig
Configuration and menu declarations in
.B Kconfig*
files.
.SS Documentation
Odysia indexes
.BR .rst ,
.BR .txt ,
and
.B .md
files.  Matching documentation excerpts are displayed with selected symbols.
Kernel-style inline documentation comments are attached to parsed declarations.
.SH DETAILS AND SOURCE
Selecting a symbol displays its kind, name, signature or type, source location,
inline documentation, child declarations, relationships, and matching external
documentation.  Related symbols and types are clickable.  The source pane shows
the indexed snippet with C and kernel-aware highlighting where applicable.
.SH SQLITE INDEXES
SQLite files contain the source root, symbols, parent-child ordering,
relationships, documentation files, source locations, signatures, types,
snippets, and inline documentation.  Loaded indexes retain programming-language
grouping because language is derived from persisted source metadata.
.PP
Database save and load are local operations.  SQLite loading runs on a worker
thread while application controls are locked.  A database does not need the
original source tree to display persisted data, although source-tree build
monitoring still requires a valid tree.
.SH SETTINGS
Odysia persists parser thread count and the detail/source font descriptions in
a GLib key file named
.IR odysia/settings.ini
below the platform user configuration directory.  On typical Linux and BSD
systems this is
.IR ${XDG_CONFIG_HOME:-$HOME/.config}/odysia/settings.ini .
On macOS it is below
.IR "$HOME/Library/Application Support/odysia" .
Windows uses the GLib user configuration directory.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ${bindir}/odysia
Installed executable.
.TP
.I ${mandir}/man1/odysia.1
Installed manual page.
.TP
.I ${datadir}/applications/org.odysia.Odysia.desktop
Linux desktop launcher.
.TP
.I ${datadir}/icons/hicolor/512x512/apps/odysia-icon.png
Linux application icon.
.TP
.I dist/Odysia.app
macOS application bundle produced by
.BR "make macos-bundle" .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B Odysia
returns zero after a normal application shutdown and nonzero if GTK application
initialization or execution fails.
.SH LIMITATIONS
The parser is designed for broad source exploration and is not a compiler or
language server.  Complex macro-generated declarations may be incomplete.
Non-C scanners are declaration-oriented heuristics.  Available symbolic UI
icons and appearance depend on the GTK theme.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR make (1),
.BR sqlite3 (1)
